---
/**
 * Advertising policy.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3). The permitted and forbidden placement
 * lists are DIRECTIVE.md §20 and AGENTS.md §4 rendered for readers; they are not authored here and
 * must not drift from those files. If §20 changes, this page changes in the same session.
 *
 * Verified for this revision, in the repository at the reviewed date: no ad script, no ad network
 * tag and no consent-management platform exists anywhere in apps/** or packages/**; the only
 * advertising-shaped artifact is packages/ui/src/primitives/AdSlot.tsx, a labelled container that
 * makes no network call and is placed on no page; apps/web/public/ads.txt does not exist; the
 * ADSENSE_SLOT_* and CMP_PROJECT_ID entries in .env.example are unset. Every claim below was
 * checked against that state rather than against an intention.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'Advertising policy — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'No advertising runs on DelayPilot today. The placement rules that will bind when it does: where an ad may appear, where it may never appear, and what is never sent to an advertiser.'

/** DIRECTIVE.md §20 — the complete permitted list. Nothing may be added to it locally. */
const permitted = [
  'On the homepage, after the complete demonstration and the first explanatory section.',
  'In the middle of a guide, after a substantial amount of the article has been read.',
  'At the end of a guide.',
  'On an airport or airline page, after the main answer that page exists to give.',
  'On a free trip result, only after the complete action checklist, with strong visual separation from it.',
]

/** DIRECTIVE.md §20 — the complete forbidden list. Nothing may be removed from it locally. */
const forbidden = [
  'Above the primary flight search.',
  'Inside any form.',
  'Between a warning and the action that answers it.',
  'Inside a passenger-rights card.',
  'Inside or next to an action checklist.',
  'Next to a "Contact airline", "Request refund", or "Save evidence" control.',
  'On sign-in, checkout, account, admin, privacy, terms, error, or status pages.',
  'Anywhere in a paid, signed-in experience.',
]
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Advertising policy</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>No advertising runs on DelayPilot.</strong> This site loads no ad script, calls no ad network,
      and carries no consent-management platform. Nothing on any page is paid placement. The rules below
      are published now, before any money is involved, because a placement policy written after the first
      advertiser arrives is a policy written to fit the advertiser.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">Reviewed <time datetime="2026-09-12">12 September 2026</time>.</p>

    <h2>The one rule the rest of this page serves</h2>

    <p>
      An ad must never be confusable with a flight, a refund, a rebooking, a claim, a contact
      control, or anything safety-related. A traveler reading this product is often tired, often
      late, and often about to spend money they will not get back. A unit that borrows the look of a
      control at that moment is not an ad — it is a trap, and no revenue justifies it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where an ad may appear</h2>

    <p>
      This list is complete. An ad may appear in these positions and nowhere else; a position that
      is not on this list is forbidden even if nothing below explicitly names it.
    </p>

    <ul>
      {permitted.map((placement) => <li>{placement}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <p>
      A trip cockpit shows at most one ad, and only after the complete action area — the checklist,
      the rights estimate, and the next useful step all come first. Advertising is what happens
      after the product has finished being useful, never instead of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where an ad may never appear</h2>

    <ul>
      {forbidden.map((placement) => <li>{placement}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <p>
      The page you are reading is itself on that list, as are the privacy and terms pages. A policy
      page carrying an ad would be advertising against the credibility of the policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Paid plans are ad-free</h2>

    <p>
      When paid plans exist, they are ad-free — not ad-reduced. A Trip Pass makes the trip it covers
      ad-free for its whole monitoring window; Plus and Family are ad-free across the entire
      signed-in experience. No paid surface carries an ad, a sponsored module, or a partner link
      dressed as a feature. No plan exists to buy today: there is no billing integration, no price
      is charged, and no purchase control is rendered anywhere on this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Consent comes before the ad, not after</h2>

    <p>
      Where consent is required for advertising or for the tracking that funds it, no ad script
      loads until that consent is recorded. Refusing is one action and no harder than accepting. A
      refusal is remembered and not re-asked on every page. There is nothing to consent to today,
      because no ad code and no consent platform exists — and a consent banner asking permission for
      tracking that does not happen would be theatre.
    </p>

    <h2>What is never sent to an advertiser</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No itinerary detail, ever.</strong> Not a flight number, not a route, not an airport pair,
        not a travel date, not a disruption state. Itinerary data never reaches ad targeting, an ad request,
        or an affiliate parameter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No booking reference.</strong> DelayPilot never asks for one, so there is none to leak
        — not to an advertiser, not to a log, not to a URL.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No personal identifiers.</strong> No email address, no display name, no account identifier,
        and no raw IP address is passed into an ad request or a targeting key.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This is the reason DelayPilot will never run behavioral targeting built on trip data, even
      though a flight-disruption audience is exactly what such a system would pay most for.
    </p>

    <h2>How a slot behaves</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Labelled.</strong> Every slot carries a visible label identifying it as advertising.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dimensions reserved.</strong> A slot holds its width and height whether or not it fills,
        so nothing on the page moves under your finger. The design system's slot container already enforces
        this: both dimensions are required, and an unfilled slot stays reserved and empty rather than
        collapsing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No refresh on a timer</strong>, and no refresh when the page quietly re-checks
        flight status in the background. An ad impression is never bought by re-rendering a
        traveler's live status view.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dropped from print.</strong> An evidence packet is a record of what happened to your flight,
        not an inventory surface.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Off in local, test, screenshot, and demo-review modes</strong>, so that no
        screenshot, test run, or demonstration ever shows an ad in a position review has not
        approved.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why there is no ads.txt file</h2>

    <p>
      An <code>ads.txt</code> file names the companies that may sell advertising inventory on a domain.
      Publishing one before any advertising integration exists would assert something about this site
      that is not true. The file ships with the ad integration itself, together with the placement tests
      that enforce everything on this page — not before it.
    </p>

    <h2>How this page changes</h2>

    <p>
      This page is rewritten, not appended to, in the same change that enables advertising. It will
      then name the network, the slots that exist, the consent mechanism, and the date the change
      took effect. Until then, the accurate summary is the first line: no advertising runs on
      DelayPilot.
    </p>

    <h2>Related policies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate disclosure</a> — commercial links and how they are
        marked.
      </li>
      <li><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> — what is collected today, and what never will be.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial policy</a> — why no advertiser can influence guidance.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
